WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:04.564
This is Queen’s Freddie Mercury, 
on September 19, 1980.

00:00:04.564 --> 00:00:08.443
 And next to him is lead guitarist, Brian May,

00:00:08.443 --> 00:00:15.088
who in July 2021 returned to find his
Kensington, London home flooded.

00:00:22.463 --> 00:00:27.926
He blamed “the basement building that has
been plaguing this area for the past 10 years.”

00:00:30.808 --> 00:00:37.495
From 2008 to 2019, 7,328 basements 
were added to homes in London.

00:00:37.495 --> 00:00:39.783
Basements not just for the rich.

00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:42.159
But the superrich.

00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:46.969
All of these dots represent private, 
underground swimming pools...

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:48.441
and cinemas...

00:00:48.441 --> 00:00:51.070
and wine cellars.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.500
Click to London’s top luxury realtors,

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.724
sort by price,

00:00:54.724 --> 00:00:58.552
and you’ll find underwater playgrounds,
and theatres.

00:00:58.552 --> 00:01:02.426
Homes like this 25 million pound one with
two basement levels,

00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:04.251
including a bar...

00:01:04.251 --> 00:01:06.011
and spa.

00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:09.930
London's elite are building underground lairs.

00:01:12.298 --> 00:01:14.725
So it's an interesting kind of conflict 
between these different

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:16.581
very, very wealthy people.

00:01:16.581 --> 00:01:19.142
Or as we said, I think in one paper, we say

00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:21.656
it's a conflict between the haves 
and the have yachts. 

00:01:24.847 --> 00:01:28.526
I'm professor Roger Burrows and 
I'm professor of cities

00:01:28.526 --> 00:01:33.156
in the School of Architecture, Planning, and Landscape 
at Newcastle University.

00:01:33.156 --> 00:01:35.580
But I'm not an architect, nor am I a planner.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.510
I'm essentially a sociologist and a statistician.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.863
Do you remember how y'all got the idea though?

00:01:40.863 --> 00:01:43.733
Was it literally just, you're 
walking down the street,

00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:46.817
you see a ton of construction trucks 
all the time,

00:01:46.817 --> 00:01:50.490
and you wonder "Why is there always 
construction on this street?"

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:51.247
And then you say,

00:01:51.247 --> 00:01:52.775
"Oh, I bet they're making a basement."

00:01:52.775 --> 00:01:53.376
Exactly.

00:01:53.376 --> 00:01:57.386
We were doing a case study of four or five
different neighborhoods in London

00:01:57.386 --> 00:02:00.794
and we were interviewing residents about 
what was happening in the neighborhood.

00:02:00.794 --> 00:02:02.883
We would notice as we were doing this,

00:02:02.883 --> 00:02:06.447
the streets with five or six of these 
excavations going on at the same time

00:02:06.447 --> 00:02:08.350
with lorries moving up and down.

00:02:12.116 --> 00:02:15.557
And indeed the people that we're interviewing
really just wanted to talk about it 

00:02:15.557 --> 00:02:18.090
because they felt that their neighborhood 
was being ruined.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:23.039
Each borough in London has a planning commission
where people have to submit renovation plans.

00:02:23.039 --> 00:02:28.045
Burrows and his coauthors searched every application
and found all the basements.

00:02:28.045 --> 00:02:29.069
And then mapped them.

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:34.174
Mostly it's concentrated around the great
parks around Hyde Park, Kensington, Chelsea

00:02:34.174 --> 00:02:35.980
and Hammersmith and Fulham.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.380
Brian May’s house, in Kensington, had an
original half-underground floor.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.066
Suddenly, his neighbors were all building
basements of an entirely different scale.

00:02:45.066 --> 00:02:49.190
A well-off neighborhood became extremely well-off.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:54.775
Transnational elites were literally burying
millions and millions of dollars in the ground.

00:02:54.775 --> 00:02:57.490
So, this isn't as much of a thing in America.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.500
Do you have a sense why that is?

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.602
Is it the historic codes in London?

00:03:01.602 --> 00:03:05.230
You can't really change the outside appearance
of a property.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.272
You can't build a loft and you can't 
extend at the back.

00:03:08.272 --> 00:03:12.695
But because of the freehold laws, 
you own all of the land under the property,

00:03:12.695 --> 00:03:14.380
so you can burrow down.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.450
A lot of it is also geological.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.720
New York is another kind of world city where
you might expect these things to be occurring.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.867
That happened a little bit in Brooklyn, 
but you're literally trying to mine a rock.

00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:28.325
The great thing about London is,
of course, it's clay.

00:03:28.325 --> 00:03:31.756
So it's very easy to dig down 
and generate these spaces.

00:03:32.826 --> 00:03:36.030
Burrows and his coauthors mapped standard,

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:36.830
large,

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.260
and mega-basements.

00:03:39.073 --> 00:03:43.060
What we call mega basements 
are three stories or more.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.483
Or, two stories, but which extend 
beyond the envelope.

00:03:47.483 --> 00:03:51.412
We find 118 of those and they're on 
a ginormous scale.

00:03:51.412 --> 00:03:54.920
This map shows their concentration 
in wealthy neighborhoods.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:59.052
The combination of incredible wealth 
and inflexible exteriors

00:03:59.052 --> 00:04:01.729
led to massive basement builds. 

00:04:01.729 --> 00:04:04.690
Wine cellars, gyms, art galleries,

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:08.769
ballrooms, panic rooms, gun stores.

00:04:09.753 --> 00:04:12.921
In the late 1800s, a ship owner named Charles Booth 

00:04:12.921 --> 00:04:15.748
started tracking inequality in London,

00:04:15.748 --> 00:04:19.879
a gradient that he mapped, 
from wealth to poverty,

00:04:19.879 --> 00:04:22.697
compiled through extensive surveys.

00:04:26.706 --> 00:04:30.584
There is now a clear, what we call, 
basement belt in London

00:04:30.584 --> 00:04:36.042
concentrated within particular neighborhoods 
in particular streets.

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:41.748
So I think it is a physical manifestation
of gross wealth inequality.

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:46.513
In the mid 2010s, London boroughs began to
curb permits for multilevel basements 

00:04:46.513 --> 00:04:50.439
because of resident concerns, like those of 
Queen's Brian May’s.

00:04:50.439 --> 00:04:52.455
The consequences are more than just flooding.

00:04:52.455 --> 00:04:55.865
Deep digging can cause structural collapses.

00:04:55.865 --> 00:04:59.555
But basement construction hasn't stopped completely.

00:04:59.555 --> 00:05:04.594
Gianluca Vialli was one of the best 
Italian strikers in the 1980s and 90s.

00:05:04.594 --> 00:05:11.423
When he moved to London in 1996, his legend
only grew as he moved into management.

00:05:11.423 --> 00:05:15.639
He helped lead Italy to win 
the Euro 2020 final.

00:05:16.124 --> 00:05:20.493
In 2020, he appeared in the lower right corner
of a video conference stream

00:05:20.493 --> 00:05:24.254
begging the Kensington and Chelsea 
Applications committee 

00:05:24.254 --> 00:05:27.578
not to let his neighbors expand their basements,

00:05:27.578 --> 00:05:30.741
after he’d lived through earlier construction.

00:05:43.965 --> 00:05:46.958
Today, his neighbor's house is for sale.

00:05:46.958 --> 00:05:50.684
It's lavish. There's also 
one major selling point:

00:05:50.684 --> 00:05:55.689
the planning board has granted permission
for a basement excavation.

